<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Yu (Southern Tang)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">937–978</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Li Houzhu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Yu was the last ruler of Southern Tang, reigning from 961 to 975, and one of the great poets of Chinese literature. Already reduced to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> tributary, his state fell when Song armies took his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in 975. He was taken north to the Song capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, where his poems of loss and exile made him a master of the ci form. He died, reportedly poisoned, in 978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-yu-southern-tang.jpg" alt="Li Yu (Southern Tang)" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wang Qi (1529 - 1612) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Yu_scth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
